name: first blood
after: 08-cutting
intro: Hints cost nothing here -- see how far you get without them. Three
intro: questions on the one file every unix has. Build each pipeline a stage at
intro: a time and look at what each stage prints before you add the next --
intro: that IS the skill.
---
setup: /etc/passwd is colon-separated. Field 1 is the username, field 7 the
setup: login shell. It is the first file you read on any machine you are handed.
q: count how many accounts exist in /etc/passwd
a: wc -l < /etc/passwd
a: cat /etc/passwd | wc -l
a: wc -l /etc/passwd
a: grep -c . /etc/passwd
out: ^[0-9]+$
ref: wc
hint: wc -l counts lines; feed it the file as an argument or on stdin
---
setup: You want just the usernames out of /etc/passwd, nothing else around them.
q: print every username in /etc/passwd, one per line
a: cut -d: -f1 /etc/passwd
ref: cut
cmp: ref
hint: -d sets the field delimiter, -f picks which field
---
setup: Same file, same field -- now put the names in order.
q: print every username in /etc/passwd, sorted alphabetically
a: cut -d: -f1 /etc/passwd | sort
ref: sort
cmp: ref
hint: pipe the previous answer's output straight into sort
